<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Leontini</h1><p class="st-meta">729 BCE – 422 BCE (approx.) · capital Leontini</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Inland Chalcidian city of eastern Sicily on a rich grain plain, whose envoy Gorgias drew Athens into Sicilian affairs in 427 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> records that Chalcidians from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocles">Theocles</a>, founded Leontini in 729 BCE, expelling the native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicels">Sicels</a> from the site. It lay on hills above the Leontine plain, one of the most fertile grain-growing areas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, a short distance inland from the sea.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Leontini's wealth came from grain. Around 610 BCE Panaetius became the first known tyrant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> there. The city fell under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates-of-gela">Hippocrates of Gela</a> around 495 BCE and later under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a>. Under pressure from Syracuse it sent the orator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgias">Gorgias</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> in 427 BCE, and his speeches won an Athenian alliance and the first Athenian expedition to Sicily; Gorgias also helped bring Sicilian rhetoric to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 422 BCE the Leontine aristocrats expelled the common people and moved to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a>, which absorbed the city. Its plight was a pretext for the great Athenian expedition of 415 BCE. Leontini was later revived as a dependent town and as Lentini survives today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/leontini.jpg" alt="Leontini" width="320" height="176"><figcaption>Image: Davide Mauro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Veduta_di_Lentini_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>729 BCE – 422 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Leontini</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Panaetius</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Leontinoi, Lentini (ancient)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-576&amp;focus=leontini" aria-label="Leontini on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5055 835 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Leontini lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5375 1091L5383 1099"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5383 1099h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5375 1091h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5375 1091h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-576&amp;focus=leontini">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

